      "beats": [
        {
          "say": "Even a correct kernel can fail a faulty test. RMS normalization looks scale-invariant when epsilon is negligible, but epsilon has a fixed magnitude. For a small input, enlarging the input while holding epsilon fixed changes its relative influence. Watch the correctly computed output move as that balance changes.",
          "start": 0.4,
          "end": 20.584,
          "duration": 20.184,
          "until": 21.233999999999998
        },
        {
          "say": "For a positive scale, multiply epsilon by the square of that scale as well. Both terms inside the square root now change together, restoring the exact-arithmetic relation. Floating-point overflow and rounding still need a numerical allowance. Validate the relation expected by the evaluator against the trusted reference before using it to reject candidates.",
          "start": 21.233999999999998,
          "end": 43.29,
          "duration": 22.056,
          "until": 43.94
        }
      ],
